>1) It would take a CRAY to run it!

Oh, bullpucky it would! Looking at display engines such as used by Bungie
(Pathways Into Darkness) and Apogee (Wolf-3D, Catacomb), it wouldn't
require a lot of CPU time.

OTOH, what _would_ be procesor-intensive would be the interaction aspects
of the game. NetHack is a step-time adventure, and was designed as such -
a lot of things would have to be changed to implement a real-time version
of Hack. Still, I think it'd be a worthwhile project.

>2) (and more important) you would loose to much info if you had a 3D
>   view. consider now you can see the entire level at once, with
>   3D you would only have a single direction perspective. 

Nonsense - there is no reason why both views could not be shown at once.
However, as I have already mentioned, implementing a real-time dungeon on
the scale of Hack would be a mammoth undertaking, but a possible one.
Limiting the player to a "view perspective" instead of seeing an entire
level/area at once increases the realism of the game. Life-sensing spells
and other detection apparati could be displayed in a "radar"-like display
windoid.


